2:title: Sketchpad: certified exact arithmetic with a small vision-language model
9:Language models are accurate on most long-arithmetic problems, but their accuracy is estimated on samples and carries no guarantee for an unseen input; tool use is exact but moves the computation outside the network. We show that exactness at all input lengths becomes a finite, checkable property of a network when each of its steps reads only a bounded view of an external working memory. In Sketchpad, Qwen3.5-0.8B with a low-rank adapter executes arithmetic procedures on a rendered sheet. Each forward pass reads a three-cell view and emits one step: a digit, a carry or borrow, and the next operation. A fixed runtime moves the view and writes no digit of a result. We prove in Lean that the model is then exact on all inputs of any length if and only if it is exact on the finite set of views the runtime presents, that no finite test suffices when steps also read earlier steps, and that the guarantee tolerates bounded numerical perturbation and extends to compositions of procedures. All 2,446 views pass on the deployment device, a laptop GPU running the model in a web browser. The certified model performs signed addition and subtraction, long multiplication and division, square roots and greatest common divisors at arbitrary length. It solved 200 of 200 pre-registered problems in the browser, and 220 of 220 sums of up to 1,000 digits and products of up to 20 digits, of which GPT-6.1 Sol, the strongest of ten other models, solved 217. A symbolic runtime built on these procedures solves, expands and factors polynomials with every number computed by the model, and a multiplicative gate on the adapter recovers the base model exactly when the sheet is not in use.
16:A guarantee for all inputs requires that behaviour on unboundedly many inputs be fixed by finitely many facts. This holds when each step of a computation reads only a bounded view of an external memory and no other state passes between steps. The steps then range over a finite set of views, and a network that is correct on each view is correct on every run of any length. The observation is classical: it underlies Moore's analysis of experiments on finite automata [@moore1956gedanken], the verification of learned recursive programs [@cai2017recursion] and a recent certificate for universal computation by language-model decoding [@lewandowski2026universal]. It also has a converse. If a step may read the steps before it, as in chain of thought, no finite test suffices.
18:Sketchpad makes this hypothesis hold by construction for a pretrained vision-language model. A runtime lays out a problem on a rendered sheet and shows the model one view at a time: three cells of the sheet. In one forward pass, the model reads the view through its vision encoder and outputs the step's results and the next operation. The runtime writes the outputs and moves the view, and nothing else passes between steps (Fig. {@fig-sheet}a). The procedures are the classical pencil-and-paper ones, and a low-rank adapter [@hu2022lora] trained on their 2,446 views teaches them to Qwen3.5-0.8B.
20:We prove in Lean that exactness on the views is equivalent to exactness at all lengths, and that the restriction to views is necessary (Section {@sec-cert}). We then certify the condition on the hardware that runs the model, a laptop GPU executing it in a web browser in 16-bit precision. All 2,446 views pass with a smallest margin of 3.02 logits, so the certificate tolerates any change of the logits smaller than 1.51. With these procedures the model adds, subtracts, multiplies and divides integers of any length and computes square roots and greatest common divisors. It answered all 220 problems of a pre-registered comparison in which the strongest of ten other models answered 217 (Section {@sec-calc}). Because certified procedures compose, a symbolic runtime built on them solves, expands and factors polynomials with every number computed by the model (Section {@sec-algebra}). Finally, a scalar gate on the adapter restores the base model exactly, and a dialogue adapter learns to call the sheet when a reply needs exact computation; it stays within 2 points of the base model on instruction following and MMLU but loses 5.8 points on fresh grade-school word problems (Section {@sec-gate}). The guarantee covers the model's steps and is relative to the runtime, whose layouts, outer loops and reference procedures are conventional code; Section {@sec-cert} states what they must satisfy.
22:::: figure {#fig-sheet src=fig1.svg pdf=fig1.pdf alt="One step of the sheet machine, the certificate over all 2,446 views, and the comparison"}
23:**Overview.** **a**, One step of the long multiplication 74,829 × 6,153 (step 33 of 56). The view (three cells, 32 × 96 pixels) enters Qwen3.5-0.8B as three image tokens after the operation token `MUL`; the outputs, read at `=` and two placeholder positions, are the digit, the carry and the next operation. Below: the operation sequence of the full product. **b**, Margins of all 2,446 views on the deployment device (Apple M4, WebGPU, 16-bit), grouped by table. The smallest margin, 3.02 logits, belongs to the addition view 5 + 6 with no incoming carry. **c**, Exact answers on the pre-registered comparison of 220 problems (sums of 10–1,000 digits and products of 2–20-digit numbers, 20 per length; Methods), overall and on the longest sums and products. Sketchpad in rose, the strongest alternative in cobalt; Qwen3.5-0.8B is shown without chain of thought, its better setting. Per-length results: Extended Data Fig. {@fig-ed1}.
30:**Model.** Qwen3.5-0.8B [@qwen35] combines a vision encoder with a 24-layer language model in which six layers use full attention and eighteen use Gated DeltaNet [@yang2025gateddeltanet]. The only trained component is a rank-16 LoRA adapter on 150 projection matrices, with 10.2 million parameters (1.2% of the model). We call it the arithmetic adapter. Section {@sec-gate} derives from it a dialogue adapter, and preliminary adapters appear only as contrasts. Device measurements were made on the GPU of an Apple M4 laptop, which we call the M4.
40:**Training.** We train the adapter directly on the tables, with cross-entropy, a hinge loss on the logit margin and a distillation penalty toward the base model on plain text [@li2017lwf] (Methods). Rows whose margin falls below 2 are replayed, and training stops when all rows have margins of at least 3. The tables are learned, not supplied by the base model. Without the adapter, Qwen3.5-0.8B passes 12 of the 2,446 views, all of them `SUM` views that copy a single digit; with eight solved views of the same table added to the prompt, it passes 158 of 2,380 (Methods).
48:Because each step is a function of its view, a check of the 2,446 views is a check of every run. This section makes the statement precise, shows that the restriction to views is necessary, and carries the check from exact arithmetic to the hardware.
52:and $v$ passes if $m(v) > 0$. The model's output $f(v)$, the argmax at each position, then equals $T(v)$ under any tie rule. A certificate records $m(v)$ for all $v \in V$.
66:Theorem {@thm-p} separates what the network must get right from what the runtime must get right. The network's part, $f = T$ on $V$, is finite, and we certify it below. The runtime's part is that reference runs present only table views, $R_T(I) \subseteq V$, and compute the intended functions. The first follows from the layouts and normalisations of Section {@sec-calc}, and the runtime asserts it at each step; the second is the correctness of the classical procedures. Neither is formalised in Lean (Methods).
85:If $a \neq y$ maximised $\tilde z$, then $\tilde z_a \le z_a + \tau < z_y - \tau \le \tilde z_y$. In a run, induction on $t$ as for Theorem {@thm-p}: while the run agrees with the reference run, its view lies in $V$ and each output is the certified one. A cached-prefix implementation of the pinned step computes the logits of the full prompt exactly (Lemma {@lem-cache}), so it is a path with $\tau = 0$ in exact arithmetic.
88:We therefore certify on the deployment path itself. In 32-bit PyTorch, the smallest margin over the 2,446 views is 3.96. On the deployment path, ONNX Runtime Web on WebGPU [@onnxruntime; @transformersjs; @webgpu] in 16-bit precision, all views pass, with smallest margins of 3.02 for addition, 6.52 for multiplication and 4.61 for the calculator tables (Fig. {@fig-cert}c). Per table, these differ from the server's smallest margins by less than one logit, whereas the 4-bit decoders of preliminary adapters deviate by up to 3 (Fig. {@fig-cert}d). The full check takes 3.5 minutes on the M4. By Theorem {@thm-transfer}, a margin of 3.02 tolerates any change of the logits smaller than 1.51. The certificate scores one view per forward pass, whereas inference batches up to 32 views, and batching changes the 16-bit logits. With every view placed in random batches of 2, 4 and 32 for the arithmetic adapter, and of 4 and 32 for the dialogue adapter of Section {@sec-gate}, no logit moved by more than 1.09 and no margin by more than 0.64. The batched paths tested therefore satisfy Theorem {@thm-transfer} with $\tau = 1.09$, below half the smallest margin of 3.02. On a different GPU, the client recomputes the certificate on request; by default it recomputes the lowest-margin tenth of each table. Appendix {@app-proofs} gives complete proofs and the corresponding Lean 4 theorems.
91:**Certification.** **a**, Sums of increasing length read only views from the 242-view addition table; nine sums of 4 to 1,024 digits reach 202 of them. **b**, The counterexample of Theorem {@thm-nofinite}: with state in the context, a decoder passes all tests up to $N$ steps and fails at step $N+1$ ($N$ = 4, 8, 16; the sink score satisfies $e^{c} = 2N + 3$, Appendix {@app-proofs}); under the pinned-sheet policy the margin is constant. **c**, Margins of all 2,446 views on the M4, by operation; dark points mark the lowest tenth, which is rechecked on each new device. **d**, Smallest margin per operation on the server (PyTorch, 32-bit) against the browser (WebGPU): 16-bit decoders lie on the identity line; 4-bit decoders of preliminary adapters deviate by up to 3 logits.
104:**Comparison.** We compared Sketchpad with ten language models on 220 pre-registered problems: sums of 10 to 1,000 digits and products of 2- to 20-digit numbers, 20 per length, with the same prompt and a budget of 32,768 output tokens (Methods). Sketchpad answered all 220. GPT-6.1 Sol, Claude Opus 5.5 and Gemini 4 Argon answered 217, 216 and 207, exact on all products, with all of their errors on sums of 300 or 1,000 digits. Five open-weight models answered between 149 and 199, failing mostly by exhausting the token budget on long sums and long products. Qwen3.5-27B answered 121, and Qwen3.5-0.8B, the base of Sketchpad, 23 without chain of thought and 3 with it (Fig. {@fig-sheet}c; Extended Data Fig. {@fig-ed1} and Table {@tbl-baselines}).
108:An algebraic derivation is a sequence of calls to certified procedures, whose inputs are transcribed from the problem and from earlier results. Its correctness then reduces to three conditions.
111:Consider $K$ calls with results $r = (r_1, \dots, r_K)$, in which the input $x_i = q_i(r_{<i})$ of call $i$ depends only on the results $r_{<i} = (r_1, \dots, r_{i-1})$ of earlier calls and the answer $g(r)$ only on $r$; let $q^*_i$, $T^*_i$ and $g^*$ be the intended inputs, functions and combining program, with results $r^*_i = T^*_i(q^*_i(r^*_{<i}))$. If every call returns its table's output on the input it received, $r_i = T_i(x_i)$, as Theorem {@thm-p} guarantees for a certified procedure, then a wrong answer requires, for some $i \le K$, a wrong table, $T_i(q^*_i(r^*_{<i})) \neq T^*_i(q^*_i(r^*_{<i}))$, or a wrong transcription, $q_i(r^*_{<i}) \neq q^*_i(r^*_{<i})$, or else a wrong combining program, $g(r^*) \neq g^*(r^*)$, and for any distribution of transcripts
115:The certificate excludes the first failure. The other two, transcribing a problem into calls and combining their results, belong to the symbolic runtime, which is conventional code, and we guard them by checking each answer before it is returned.
129:where $W$ is the frozen weight, $A$ and $B$ are the adapter matrices and $s$ is the LoRA scale (Fig. {@fig-gate}a). At $g = 0$ each branch contributes an exact floating-point zero, provided it is finite, so the gated model is the base model; we confirmed this bit for bit for logits, hidden states and cached states (Methods). At $g = 1$, the certificate on the M4 reproduces all 2,446 margins.
133:Against criteria fixed before training (Methods), the dialogue adapter invokes the sheet on held-out phrasings with precision 0.994 and recall 0.989, against 0.971 and 0.809 for the base model prompted with four examples. It writes the correct computation for 92.8% of word problems, 96.7% of equations and 98.3% of expressions embedded in prose. On the benchmark items used during development, it stayed within 1.6 points of the base model on IFEval (−1.5 ± 2.0), GSM8K [@cobbe2021gsm8k] (−1.6 ± 3.4) and MMLU [@hendrycks2021mmlu] (−1.4 ± 1.6), each less than one standard error from the criterion of −2. Because it was the fifth candidate scored on those items, we rescored it on items not used before, against predictions fixed in advance. MMLU held, at −2.0 ± 1.1 on 1,000 questions, but GSM8K did not: −5.8 ± 1.7 on the 1,069 remaining test problems, so the retention criterion is not confirmed for multi-step word problems. The loss appears both where the adapter calls the sheet and where it answers directly, which places it in multi-step reasoning rather than in the sheet. The same weights at $g = 1$ execute the arithmetic procedures: on the M4, all 2,446 views pass, with smallest margins of 6.67 for addition, 6.53 for multiplication and 4.56 for the calculator tables. Across recipes, IFEval and GSM8K trade off against each other (Fig. {@fig-gate}b). On-policy distillation removes most of the trade-off, and reinforcement learning reintroduces it unless anchored to the supervised checkpoint (Extended Data Table {@tbl-chat}).
141:The certificate establishes what the model outputs on each view, not where in the network the output is decided. We applied the logit lens [@nostalgebraist2020logitlens; @belrose2023tunedlens] to the arithmetic adapter at the first output position: with $h_\ell$ the residual state after depth $\ell$, and $W_U$ and $\mathrm{norm}$ the final unembedding and normalisation,
155:A pretrained vision-language model of 0.8 billion parameters, restricted to a bounded view of its working memory, can be certified exact on inputs of every length by checking 2,446 views on the device that runs it. On the longest sums of our comparison, where the strongest frontier models made silent errors, its answers carry this guarantee, and the same certified procedures support symbolic algebra and sit behind an exact gate in a dialogue model.
157:The certificate covers the model's steps. The runtime that sequences them, the transcription of a problem into calls and the translation of a word problem into a computation are conventional code or uncertified model behaviour; the dialogue adapter mistranslates about 7% of held-out word problems, and on fresh grade-school problems it reasons less well than the base model, 5.8 points below it on GSM8K. In algebra, the answer checks guard these parts without being complete: they verify the roots found, not that all roots were found, and evaluation at a point is a spot check. The guarantee holds for the execution path on which the margins were measured, and on a new GPU it requires the full check, of which the client by default repeats only the lowest-margin tenth. Inputs must be rendered in the model's glyph set, the algebra is limited to polynomials of low degree in one variable and linear systems of up to three equations, and a step takes about 0.1 s on a laptop GPU.
159:Theorem {@thm-nofinite} bears on methods that estimate accuracy on samples. When a step reads the steps before it, as in chain of thought and in most formats studied for length generalisation, no finite test certifies the model, however long the tested inputs. Context disciplines such as the inductive scratchpad [@abbe2024globality] and PENCIL [@yang2025pencil] bound what a step reads in order to generalise. The pinned-sheet policy, like the decoding of Lewandowski et al. [@lewandowski2026universal], is the limit in which a step reads only a bounded view, and Theorem {@thm-p} shows that in this limit a finite check suffices. The price is a runtime that sequences the steps, and the gain is a guarantee that does not depend on which inputs were tested.
165:The language model of Qwen3.5-0.8B [@qwen35] has 24 layers with hidden size 1,024; layers 4, 8, …, 24 (numbered from 1) use full attention (8 query and 2 key-value heads), the others Gated DeltaNet [@yang2025gateddeltanet]. The LoRA adapter (rank 16, scale 2) is applied to the query, key, value and output projections of the 6 attention layers (24 matrices), the two input projections and the output projection of the 18 Gated DeltaNet layers (54) and the three projections of each of the 24 feed-forward blocks (72): 150 matrices and 10,223,616 parameters, against 873,438,784 in the base model. For deployment the model is exported to ONNX, with a 16-bit vision encoder and language model and 4-bit token embeddings (16-bit, looked up inside the decoder graph, for the dialogue adapter); the adapter is folded into the decoder graph. Two additional graph inputs implement the gate and the steering intervention, and one additional output returns hidden states.
173:Each training step draws a batch $R$ of rows from every table, together with rows replayed from the last certificate. With $z_k(v;\theta)$ the logits at output position $k$ of row $v$, $K_v \in \{1, 3\}$ the number of output positions and $m_k(v;\theta)$ the margin at position $k$, the term inside the minimum in ({@eq-margin}), the loss is
180:with hinge target $h = 4$ and $\lambda = 1$, where $x$ is a passage of $n = 128$ tokens of plain text and $p_0$ the base model [@li2017lwf]. Every 100 steps the full certificate is computed and rows with margins below $h/2 = 2$ are replayed until the next; training stops when every row has a margin of at least 3. The arithmetic adapter was initialised from a preliminary adapter certified on the addition and multiplication tables, with the subtraction, estimation and decrement tables added. It passed every row at step 1,200 and was stopped at step 1,600 (19.8 minutes on one H100 GPU; learning rate on a cosine schedule from $2\times10^{-4}$ to $2\times10^{-5}$ over 4,000 steps). Its plain-text perplexity is 1.0048 times that of the base model.
184:Margins are computed over the full vocabulary of 248,320 tokens. The server certificate uses PyTorch in 32-bit floating point. The device certificate runs the deployed files (transformers.js 4.2.0 [@transformersjs], ONNX Runtime Web on WebGPU) through the inference code path and batched prompt form, and records every margin with the device, library version, file hashes and glyph set (Appendix {@app-cert}). Padding the addition prompt to the batched form changed no margin by more than 0.0018. Two device certificates, with and without the gate, agree on all margins to $10^{-3}$. The certificate is bound to the model files by their hashes (Appendix {@app-cert}). The batch check scores each of the 2,446 views alone and inside a batch of randomly chosen other views, in the padded prompt form of inference, and records the largest change of any logit over the full vocabulary and of the view's margin. For each batch size, one random partition covers all views once: sizes 2, 4 and 32 for the arithmetic adapter, and 4 and 32 for the dialogue adapter. The check samples batch compositions; it does not bound all of them.
186:The base-model control scores the same 2,446 views with the base model, in 32-bit PyTorch and the prompt form of the server certificate. Zero-shot, it passes 12 views, all of them `SUM` views, and 1.1% of output positions are correct. With eight solved views of the same table (fixed seed), each followed by its outputs, prefixed to every query, it passes 35 of the 2,380 views not among the examples, with outputs read at the placeholder positions, and 158 when each output after the first is read after the correct earlier outputs, the form the examples show (45.8% of positions); no table passes completely in any setting.
196:For square roots, write $a = A \cdot 100^k$ with $1 \le A < 100$. The runtime takes $c = 4$ if $A < 1.5625$, $c = 2$ if $A < 6.25$, $c = 1$ if $A < 25$ and $c = 5$ otherwise, comparing the first six digits of $a$, aligned to pairs, with 15625, 62500 and 250000. Then $c \sqrt A$ lies in $[2.5, 5)$ if $A < 25$ and in $[25, 50)$ otherwise, so the root of $c^2 a$ begins with 25 to 49 when it has more than one digit and is a single digit from 2 to 4 otherwise; we also verified this for every radicand from 1 to 999,999. Division scales by $f = \lfloor 10/(d_0+1) \rfloor \in \{1, 2, 3, 5\}$, and $f (d_0 + 1) \le 10$ keeps the scaled divisor at the same length. The scalings use the multiplication procedure; the remainder of a division is unscaled by multiplying by $10/f$ and dropping the last digit ($f = 2, 5$) or by an exact division by 3, and the root by multiplying by $10/c$ or $100/c$ and dropping one or two digits. The runtime's choice of scale factors and initial guesses affects the number of steps but not the result, since every guess is at least the true digit and is lowered only by certified steps. The cost model was fitted on batches of 1 to 32 views on the M4.
208:The gate multiplies each adapter branch before it is added to the frozen projection. At $g = 0$ the branches are still evaluated, which adds the adapter's 1.2% of parameters to each forward pass. The identity test compared the base, ungated and gated graphs bit for bit under ONNX Runtime 1.31.1 (the Python package) on CPU, on 100 chat prompts (first user turns of the No Robots test split [@norobots2023]; one prefill and 24 greedy steps each, 2,500 forward passes) and on 2,688 table views: the 2,446 views and the 242 addition views again in the padded prompt form. Every logit, hidden state and cached state of the gated graph equals that of the base model at $g = 0$ and that of the ungated adapter at $g = 1$, and greedy completions of the 100 chat prompts are identical.
212:The training data comprise about 3,150 synthetic dialogues, about 30% of which should not invoke the sheet, and 547 problems from the GSM8K training split, solved by the base model with each calculation replaced by a call to the sheet. A call is a Markdown code block labelled `sheet`, with one calculator expression or equation per line; generation stops when the block closes, the runtime executes the block and appends one result line per expression, and generation resumes, with at most two blocks per reply. Every training step, in the supervised, distillation and reinforcement-learning stages alike, includes a batch of table rows under the objective ({@eq-loss}), with low-margin rows replayed, and every stage was required to end with all 2,446 rows passing. The held-out set contains 780 items from unseen templates. Invocations are scored by execution. Criteria were fixed before training: precision and recall of invocation of at least 0.95; correct invocations for at least 90% of word problems and 95% of equations; IFEval, GSM8K (250 test problems) and MMLU (500 test questions; both subsets drawn with seed 0) within 2 points of the base model. GSM8K is evaluated at $g = 1$ with the sheet in the loop; in these evaluations, and in the reward, the blocks are executed by a reference implementation of the procedures in exact integer arithmetic rather than by the model, which by Theorem {@thm-p} returns the same results. The dialogue adapter was the fifth candidate scored on the same benchmark items. We therefore rescored it and the base model on the 1,069 GSM8K test problems and 1,000 MMLU test questions not used before, with predictions and a stopping rule fixed in advance (GSM8K −1.5 within [−4.5, +1.5]; MMLU −1.0 within [−3.0, +1.0]; failure below −2 by more than one standard error). GSM8K fell outside its band: 0.491 against 0.549 (139 problems gained, 201 lost). The adapter called the sheet on 619 problems and solved 327, where the base model solved 368 of the same; on the other 450 it solved 198 against 219. MMLU stayed inside its band, at 0.463 against 0.483. The benchmark differences are reported with the standard error of a paired difference, $\sqrt{n_{+} + n_{-}}/n$ for $n_{+}$ and $n_{-}$ items gained and lost out of $n$. As an alternative to training the decision, we switched the gate within a reply: the base model, prompted with four examples, writes the prose at $g = 0$, and an adapter writes only the block at $g = 1$; two preliminary dialogue adapters served as block writers (Fig. {@fig-gate}b,c). The switch is exact, but invocation inherits the base model's recall of 0.809, and the sheet prompt lowered the base model's GSM8K accuracy by 34 to 37 points. Reinforcement learning used GRPO with groups of eight samples per task and four tasks per step, a reward that requires a finished reply quoting the executed result and penalises repeated or excess invocations, and a KL penalty toward the supervised checkpoint on every generated token; it ran for 200 steps on one H100 GPU.
220:The comparison was pre-registered with its scoring rule. Problems were generated with a fixed seed; every model received the prompt "What is a + b? Give the exact integer. Put the final answer in \boxed{}." (with × for products). The answer was the last boxed integer, and a reply that exhausted the budget or gave no answer was scored as wrong. Qwen3.5-0.8B and Qwen3.5-27B (FP8 release) ran under vLLM with the recommended sampling settings and budgets of 32,768 tokens with chain of thought and 4,096 without. GPT-6.1 Sol (`gpt-6.1-sol`, reasoning effort high), Claude Opus 5.5 (`claude-opus-5-5`, effort high) and Gemini 4 Argon (`gemini-4-argon`, thinking level high) were accessed through their APIs on 31 October 2026 with default sampling and a budget of 32,768 output tokens including reasoning. Five open-weight models, DeepSeek-V4-Pro (`deepseek-v4-pro:0813`), Mistral-Large-4, Gemma 4 31B, GLM-5.3 and gpt-oss-120b, ran through the Ollama cloud API on 31 October 2026 with reasoning enabled at the highest available setting and the same budget; Kimi K3 was piloted and excluded on cost. Each model was run once per problem, so a difference of a few problems between two models is within sampling variation. Sketchpad ran all 220 problems in batched lockstep in PyTorch, 64,700 views in 1,001 forward passes.
224:**Neural arithmetic.** The Neural GPU learned binary addition and multiplication that generalise beyond the training length [@kaiser2016neuralgpu; @price2016neuralgpu], and specialised arithmetic units followed [@trask2018nalu; @madsen2020nau]. For transformers, positional encodings and data formats determine length generalisation [@nogueira2021limitations; @anil2022length; @lee2024teaching; @zhou2024raspl; @zhou2024robust; @mcleish2024abacus; @cho2024coupling], and fine-tuned models reach high accuracy on bounded arithmetic [@liu2023goat; @yang2023mathglm], with division and gcd remaining difficult [@shen2024revorder; @charton2024gcd; @kasliwal2026division]. Neural cellular automata and differentiable transducers trained from scratch add and multiply far beyond their training lengths [@wei2026nca; @wei2026ncaadd; @dfst2025]. Mechanistic analyses find addition circuits correct on almost all inputs [@quirke2024verified]. Exactness has been proved under idealised training [@backdeluca2025exact], for small recurrent networks that add inputs of any length after training with a description-length objective [@lan2022mdl], and for a purpose-built transformer parameterisation that evaluates Boolean circuits of any depth [@ito2026circuits]. Sketchpad differs in using a pretrained vision-language model, in covering division, square roots and gcd, and in certifying the trained weights by enumeration, where these works estimate accuracy on samples or prove exactness for idealised, small or purpose-built networks.
230:**Verification.** Neural network verification typically bounds outputs over continuous input regions [@katz2017reluplex]; here the input set is finite, as in computer-assisted proofs of a small transformer's accuracy on a task of fixed input size [@gross2024compact], except that a finite set of views covers inputs of every length. Our theorems specialise results on the identification of finite machines [@moore1956gedanken; @chow1978testing], the verification of learned recursive programs [@cai2017recursion] and certified universal computation by language models [@schuurmans2024universal; @lewandowski2026universal]. Lewandowski et al. likewise restrict each decoding step to a fixed prompt and a local pair of symbols, and check a pretrained model's output on all 1,857 rules of a universal system by exact match at temperature zero; Sketchpad trains a small model to meet the condition and certifies margins on the deployment path, which Theorem {@thm-transfer} makes robust to that path's numerical differences. Discrete neural algorithmic reasoning forces a processor through finite predefined states, so that its learned algorithms can be proved correct on any input [@rodionov2025dnar]; it trains processors from scratch, where Sketchpad certifies a pretrained model. The context discipline resembles the inductive scratchpad [@abbe2024globality] and PENCIL [@yang2025pencil]. Certification from examples can be hard in general and length-generalisation bounds can be uncomputable [@backdeluca2026certification; @yang2026lengthbounds]; the pinned-sheet policy avoids these obstacles by making the input set finite.
240:The model, adapter, certificate and analysis data are available on Hugging Face (`mohsenvand/sketchpad-0.8b-preview`). The runtime, the algebra, the baselines and the sources of this paper are available under the Apache 2.0 licence at https://github.com/NeoVand/sketchpad. The Lean proofs are in `paper/lean/` of the same repository (Appendix {@app-proofs}).
262:| **All** | | | **2,446** | **3.96** | **3.02** |
277:**Cost of the algebra benchmark.** Views, forward passes after batching and seconds under the cost model, default check, for the textbook and evaluation routes. The first eleven problems are those of Fig. {@fig-algebra}c. The last three have irrational roots, which the textbook route returns as radicals and the evaluation route only as decimal bounds, so they have no comparable evaluation entry and were not measured on the M4.
317:**Comparison.** Problems answered exactly, of 20 per cell; in brackets, the number of replies that exhausted the token budget. Qwen3.5-27B is the FP8 release; all other models ran with reasoning at their highest available setting, accessed on 31 October 2026. The errors of GPT-6.1 Sol and Claude Opus 5.5 were completed replies well within the budget, differing from the correct sum in 1 to 637 digits; 9 of the 13 errors of Gemini 4 Argon exhausted the budget. Most errors of the open-weight models exhausted the budget, except those of Gemma 4 31B, 46 of whose 64 errors were incorrect answers in completed replies. Qwen3.5-27B exhausted the budget in 93 of its 99 failures, and Qwen3.5-0.8B with chain of thought in all of its 217.
